      <!-- SECTION 4: SOLVENTS, ACIDS & ALKALIES -->
      <section class="section">
        <h2 id="solvents-acids-alkalies">4. Solvents, Acids &amp; Alkalies in Soil Dissolution</h2>
        
        <p>
          Specific cleaning categories require chemical reactions that extend beyond simple surfactant encapsulation. Hard surface cleaning requires the direct chemical dissolution of inorganic mineral scales, oxidized grease layers, and cross-linked floor coatings. Solvents, acids, and alkalies act as raw chemical reagents that physically break down molecular bonds inside the soil matrix, rendering them water-soluble or highly dispersible.
        </p>

        <h3>4.1 Hansen Solubility Parameters in Solvent Degreasing</h3>
        <p>
          Solvents such as Isopropyl Alcohol (IPA), Ethylene Glycol Monobutyl Ether (Butyl Cellosolve), and Propylene Glycol Ethers are added to glass and kitchen cleaners to dissolve heavy grease. Formulators select solvents using <strong>Hansen Solubility Parameters</strong>, which measure the energy required to dissolve a solute based on three cohesive forces: dispersion (&delta;<sub>d</sub>), polar molecular interactions (&delta;<sub>p</sub>), and hydrogen bonding (&delta;<sub>h</sub>). By matching the solvent's Hansen parameters to those of the greasy soil, the solvent rapidly penetrates, dissolves, and evaporates without leaving residue or streaks.
        </p>

        <h3>4.2 Inorganic Mineral Dissolution: Acid Chemistry</h3>
        <p>
          Toilet bowls and bathroom tiles develop stubborn scale composed of calcium carbonate ($CaCO_3$) and metal oxides. Acid cleaners dissolve these deposits via proton-driven neutralization:
        </p>
        <div style="background: #f1f5f9; padding: 16px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); font-family: monospace; font-size: 1.1rem; text-align: center; margin: 15px 0; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
          CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) + 2H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Ca<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)
        </div>
        <p>
          Strong mineral acids like <strong>Hydrochloric Acid (HCl)</strong> dissolve scale extremely fast but pose high corrosion risks to stainless steel and skin. Formulators utilize organic acids such as <strong>Citric Acid</strong> and <strong>Sulfamic Acid</strong> combined with nonionic surfactants. These organic acids act as mild chelators and scale-dissolving agents, providing safer fuming profiles and high compatibility with plumbing systems.
        </p>

        <h3>4.3 Fat Saponification &amp; Silicate Passivation: Alkaline Chemistry</h3>
        <p>
          Oven cleaners and heavy-duty degreasers use strong alkalies like <strong>Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH)</strong> or <strong>Potassium Hydroxide (KOH)</strong> to trigger <strong>Saponification</strong>. Saponification chemically converts insoluble fatty acid triglycerides (grease) into soluble sodium soaps (glycerol and soap carboxylate salts). To protect soft metal substrates like aluminum from high-pH corrosion, <strong>Sodium Metasilicates</strong> are added to form a protective silicate passivation monolayer on the metal surface, preventing caustic etching.
        </p>
      </section>

